X86 x86-userword-handoff-fixed
(* The user-word handoff under the fix, on x86.

   Paired with x86-userword-handoff-main, this is the row that answers the
   question directly: both are Never, so on x86 the slot-claim change neither
   helps nor harms the user-word feature. The ordering the user-word column
   depends on was already supplied by TSO, and the fix does not take it away.

   The difference between the two architectures is worth stating plainly. On
   ARM the fix CLOSES a pre-existing hole (see the arm64 pair); on x86 there
   was no hole and there is no regression. Either way the direction is never
   worse. *)
{ uw=7; tid=3; lce=5; }
 P0           | P1              ;
 MOV [uw],$9  | MOV EDX,$1      ;
 MOV [tid],$0 | XCHG [lce],EDX  ;
 MOV [lce],$0 | MOV EAX,[uw]    ;
exists (1:EDX=0 /\ 1:EAX=7)
